it's no secret that in the late 19th  century the great powers didn't get  along yet these nations despite their  mutual distrust and rivalries all work  together to suppress china's boxer  rebellion at the turn of the 20th  century which raises the question why  why did so many great powers work  together despite the fact that they  hated each other so first a brief  overview the boxer rebellion began in  1899 and came about as a result of the  qing government's weakness against  foreign colonial powers these powers had  received major concessions from the  chinese including economic domination in  several provinces ownership of important  ports freedom to proselytise throughout  all of china and europeans were also  exempt from chinese law all of these  were embarrassing for china but the last  two in particular were saw points for  the chinese people it wasn't long until  some of them took matters into their own  hands and began to want themselves and  retaliate against foreigners in china  against the wishes of the chinese  government these rebels who were known  outside of china as boxers because of  their knowledge of martial arts soon  raised an army and laid siege to the  foreign quarter of beijing in response  an international coalition of countries  mostly great powers arrived to put down  the rebellion which they did so why did  so many nations get involved and why did  they put aside their differences to do  so the first reason why all of these  nations got involved was because their  citizens were in immediate danger and  many feared a bloodbath there was a fear  that the europeans and christians in  china were going to be slaughtered and  so for the wider publics of the great  powers as well as the churches in their  countries it was a moral imperative that  these people be saved the second reason  was prestige having your people harmed  wasn't just bad for them but it also  would have hurt the nation's  international standing the third reason  was money although in varying degrees  for each country the united states  wanted china to open up to trade in the  same way that japan had done in the mid  19th century because for america china  was primarily a market for exports  britain france russia and germany wanted  to maintain their monopolies and  privileges in the parts of china that  they had influence over japan's  involvement was also about making it  clear that china was in their backyard  and that they wish to be treated as a  great power equal to the others whereas  austria-hungary's and italy's motives  were much more about chinese calling  stations these two didn't have any  colonies of their own in asia and so to  keep that part of the world open to them  they needed access to the neutral  chinese culling facilities these were  open to any country ships and so unlike  those controlled by another imperial  power there was no risk of them being  closed off italy had another reason and  that was that it didn't have any of the  same privileges in china that the other  european powers had they had tried to  get them earlier but the qing rulers  told them no and so if italy was to be  seen as an established power it needed  to obtain some and so together they all  fought to defeat the boxer rebellion and  you would hope that working together  would create a sense of brotherhood  between the nations involved to decrease  tensions but that's not how early 20th  century great power politics worked and  just over a decade later it was time for  the first world war  i hope you enjoyed this episode with a  special thanks to my patrons james  bissonette kelly moneymaker mr wolfe  skye chappelle adam stalter jordan  longley rod d martin gareth turner wyan  hockey jerry lamdin spencer lightfoot  corso wolff boogerly wiggly marvin  cassar winston k wood gustav swan marcus  asner captain cydog aaron the white  matthew shipley robert wetzel alex  schwinn maggie paxkowski the mcwhopper  anthony beckett coppertone spinning  through plates charles the first ben  evanson scottish trekkie and words about  books podcast
 

